! Floating-point diagnostic jb_af1c, routines F207C "CHANGED
! REGISTERS" and F207D "INDICATOR TEST" for LOAD DOUBLE
! (docs/jb_af1c_floating-point.pdf p.90, octal 014524.00-014542.40;
! they share one exit, so both are here):
!   C1 "$SB 5-7 ones to zeros": CM1111 sets the whole sign byte to
!     ones, then DL(U),FPK1 -- and the flag bits, bit positions 5-7 of
!     the sign byte register, must come back ZERO. Ref. Manual p.96:
!     "The flag bits, bit positions 5-7 of the sign byte register, are
!     set to zero." That is what makes DL differ from DLWF, which
!     takes the addressed operand's flags instead, and it is checked
!     here with a 3-bit CT0011 at 10.5.
!   D1 the data flag indicators cleared: BB1 on $TF, $UF and $VF sets
!     all three to one (branch on bit and set to one), then DL(U) of
!     FPK2 -- whose flag bits are zero -- and BTF/BUF/BVF,SERS must
!     not branch.
!   D2 the same set to ones: Z,$IND clears them, DL(U) of FPK1 --
!     whose flag bits are ones -- and BZTF/BZUF/BZVF,SERS must not
!     branch. The indicators follow the ADDRESSED operand's flags
!     whatever DL does to the sign byte's copy of them, which is why
!     D2 passes while C1 expects zeros.
!
! Substitutions (SSIP framework, real listed addresses): SERS (octal
! 1304) -> FAIL halt, SEN (octal 1310) -> scratch, PASS -> the
! routines' exit at 014542.40 (the listing's "B,$+1.", followed by
! B,F207C / SIC,SEN0+.32 / B,SSW). Constants from the pool p.176.
!
! Instructions: BD, CM1111 (connect to memory) on the sign byte,
! DL(U), CT0011 over a 3-bit field at 10.5, BB1 on $TF/$UF/$VF,
! BTF/BUF/BVF and BZTF/BZUF/BZVF, Z($IND), SIC, BRGZ.
! verify_fp_arith_f207cd_listing.py checks every word bit-for-bit.
!
! Observed: C1 halted at SERS (SEN = 014530.00) -- and the sign byte
! was RIGHT. Dumping word 10 at the halt gave 0xf8..., bits 0-3 the
! ones CM1111 left, bit 4 the sign of FPK1, bits 5-7 zero exactly as
! DL should leave them. The fault was the CT0011 reading word 10 one
! half-word later, before the DL that writes it had retired: an
! ordering hazard, not arithmetic. Putting three half-words between
! the same two instructions made it pass, which is what identified it.
! The look-ahead's store-close-to-fetch wait compares a fetch address
! against earlier levels' STORE targets, and storeTarget() reports
! only stores to storage -- an FP load writes the accumulator, not
! memory -- so nothing held this fetch. A level fetching one of the
! E-box's own registers (words 7-15) now waits for the newest earlier
! level whatever it does; see programs/ebox_register_close_to_fetch.strap.
! D1 and D2 passed once C1 did.

! -- SSIP framework substitutes --
        SLC, (8)1304.0
SERS    BEW, $
        SLC, (8)1310.0
SEN     DD(BU,64,8), 0

! -- constants (pool p.176) --

        SLC, (8)22006.0
FPK1    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777777
        SLC, (8)22007.0
FPK2    DD(BU,64,8), (8)1777777777777777777760

! -- code (verbatim, at the listing's addresses) --
        SLC, (8)14524.0
FLD1C   BD, $+.32   ! F207C1 / SAP: $SB 5-7 ones to zeros
        CM1111(BU,8), 10.0   ! set the whole sign byte to ones
        DL(U), FPK1
        CT0011(BU,3), 10.5   ! the flag bits must be zero
        SIC, SEN
        BRGZ, SERS
        BB1, $TF, $+1.   ! F207D1: data flags TF UF VF set
        BB1, $UF, $+1.   ! F207D1: data flags TF UF VF set
        BB1, $VF, $+1.   ! F207D1: data flags TF UF VF set
        DL(U), FPK2   ! whose flag bits are zero
        SIC, SEN
        BTF, SERS   ! error if not set to zero
        SIC, SEN
        BUF, SERS   ! error if not set to zero
        SIC, SEN
        BVF, SERS   ! error if not set to zero
        Z, $IND   ! F207D2: data flags TF UF VF set
        DL(U), FPK1   ! whose flag bits are ones
        SIC, SEN
        BZTF, SERS   ! error if not set to ones
        SIC, SEN
        BZUF, SERS   ! error if not set to ones
        SIC, SEN
        BZVF, SERS   ! error if not set to ones
PASS    BEW, $                  ! 014542.40: the routine's exit ("B,$+1.")
        END, FLD1C
